tel 1.0

base vivisection
import soundness

info   a JVM debugger over the wire: launch or attach, break, step, inspect and evaluate, with the protocol as typed values
detail
    _Vivisection_ debugs one JVM from another over the Java Debug Wire Protocol (JDWP), spoken
    directly over a socket with no dependency on `com.sun.jdi`. A **debuggee** is either
    launched under the JDWP agent (`Debuggee`) or attached to where it already listens
    (`Debugger`); either one's `session` lends a `Debug` capability to a block, and inside that
    block the program is suspended and resumed, its threads listed and named, breakpoints set by
    source line or by class and method, threads stepped, frames and their variables read, and
    expressions evaluated in the stopped program. The VM's own notifications arrive as typed
    `Jdwp.Event`s on a stream rather than as callbacks.

    The protocol underneath is modelled as values. Every identifier the VM hands out is a
    `Jdwp.Ref` tagged by its kind — `ThreadId`, `FrameId`, `MethodId`, `ObjectId` and the rest —
    so one can never be passed where another is wanted, yet all cost nothing at runtime. A stop
    is a `Halt`: a capability lent to a breakpoint or step handler for exactly as long as the
    thread stands suspended, which is the only time the identifiers it hands out are valid.
    The session itself, `Debug`, is confined to its block by capture checking, so it cannot be
    used after the debuggee has gone.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import probates.awaitProbate
    import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem
    import threads.virtualThreads
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    def names(classpath: Text): List[Text] = supervise:
      val target: Debuggee = Debuggee(sh"java -classpath $classpath com.example.Main", port = 0)

      target.session:
        debug.threads().map(debug.name(_))
    ```

    The command is launched with the agent option inserted after the executable, the session
    waits for the agent to listen, attaches, and tears everything down — socket closed, process
    killed — when the block ends, whether it returns or fails. The `debug` accessor names the
    lent session from context, as `monitor` does for a supervision scope.

    A breakpoint takes a *handler*, run on the session's dispatcher task with a `Halt` lent for
    the stop; the thread resumes when the handler returns unless the handler calls `remain()`.
    Variables are recovered in the form the programmer wrote them — captured values un-flattened
    from their synthetic fields, `var`s unboxed from their ref cells, lazy vals read but never
    forced — and stepping is *logical*: a step over a line containing an inlined call passes
    over the whole line, a step into it stops at the first line of the inlined body, and
    bridges, forwarders, accessors and the JDK's own frames are never shown.

    The library is three components, each exported to the `soundness` umbrella:

    - `vivisection.core` — `Debuggee`, `Debugger`, `Debug`, `Halt`, `Variable`, `Breakpoint`,
      `SourceBreakpoint`, the identifier aliases and the `Jdwp` protocol model. JVM only: it
      debugs JVMs over TCP and launches them through guillotine.
    - `vivisection.eval` — `Evaluator`, `Purview`, `Namer` and the `evaluator` extension on
      `Halt`, which compile an expression against the debuggee's classpath in this JVM, inject
      the classfiles over the wire and run them in the debuggee. Kept out of `core` so a caller
      who only inspects variables does not depend on the Scala compiler.
    - `vivisection.dap` — `Dap`, the Debug Adapter Protocol adapter: `Dap.listen` serves a
      session to an editor over `Content-Length`-framed JSON on the standard streams, and the
      `Dap` object holds the protocol's message types. The `demo` component's `DapServer` is a
      runnable main class for pointing an editor at.

    One name is deliberately *not* exported: `Variable`, since ambience already publishes a
    `Variable` (an environment variable) into `soundness`. A debugger variable is reached as
    `vivisection.Variable`, or by excluding the other with `import soundness.{Variable as _, *}`.

    A session needs a `Monitor` (it runs its writer, reader and dispatcher as tasks, so it lives
    inside `supervise`), a `Probate`, a `Tactic[Debugger.Error]`, `Diagnostics`, and the socket
    and logging givens shown in the sample. A launch additionally needs an `Online`, a
    `Socket.Backend`, a `WorkingDirectory` and an `Environment` for the forked process. None of
    these are choice packages of this library; they are imported by name from their own.

    When the debuggee is the program's own classes under a host such as a test runner, take the
    classpath from the classloader of one of those classes
    (`LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Main.type])`) rather than the `java.class.path` property,
    which names the host's jars: a debuggee launched on the wrong classpath never loads the
    class a breakpoint waits for, and the session waits forever.

refer vivisection.Debuggee
refer vivisection.Debugger
refer vivisection.Debug
refer vivisection.Halt
refer vivisection.Variable
refer vivisection.Jdwp
refer vivisection.Evaluator
refer vivisection.Dap

entry .Debuggee   a JVM to launch under the JDWP agent and debug: a command, a port and whether to suspend at start
  detail
      A `Debuggee` describes a program to run under the debug agent. `target.session { … }`
      forks `command` with `-agentlib:jdwp=transport=dt_socket,server=y,…` inserted just after
      the executable, drains the child's standard output and error from the moment of the fork
      (so a chatty debuggee can never block against a full pipe), waits for the agent to listen,
      attaches, negotiates identifier sizes and lends a `Debug` for the block. When the block
      ends the process is killed, its drains and exit watcher awaited, and the socket closed.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      def version(classpath: Text): Jdwp.Version = supervise:
        Debuggee(sh"java -classpath $classpath com.example.Main", port = 0).session:
          debug.version()
      ```

      `port` is the TCP port the agent listens on, `5005` by default. A `port` of `0` lets the
      agent bind a free port of its own choosing, which the session reads from the agent's
      `Listening for transport dt_socket at address: N` line on the debuggee's standard output
      before attaching, so no other process can take the port in between; this is what the DAP
      adapter's `launch` uses. `suspended` (`true` by default) makes the VM wait before running
      `main`, which is what a breakpoint in initialisation — or any breakpoint placed before the
      program runs — needs: set the breakpoints, then `debug.resume()`.

      The launch degrades to a `Debugger.Error` with reason `Disconnected` and a detail of
      _could not launch_, _the port was invalid_ or _never listened_ (after about ten seconds
      without the agent answering). A launched session has a `Debug.Console` at
      `debug.console`, over the child's output and exit status; an attached one has none.
  refer vivisection.Debugger
  refer vivisection.Debug
  entry #command   the command to launch, without the agent option, which is inserted at launch
  entry #port   the TCP port the agent listens on, or `0` to let the agent choose a free one
  entry #suspended   whether the VM waits at startup until the session resumes it; `true` by default
  entry .Sessional   the `aperture.Sessional` which launches a debuggee and lends a `Debug`
    detail
        The instance behind `debuggee.session { … }`, with `Debuggee` as its `Self` and
        `Debug^` as its `Result`. It takes its contextual values — `Online`, `Monitor`,
        `Probate`, `Socket.Backend`, `Every[Socket.Option.Tcp]`, `Tactic[Async.Error]`,
        `WorkingDirectory`, `Environment`, the `Socket.Event` and `Exec.Event` loggers,
        `Tactic[Debugger.Error]` and `Diagnostics` — at construction, and `.sessional` builds
        one from scope. The process is launched with the `Environment` given: the invocation's
        own in a tool, or `environments.javaBaseEnvironment` for the JVM's.
    entry #session   forks the debuggee, attaches, and runs the block with a `Debug` lent to it
        Converts a launch failure (`Exec.Error`), a bad port (`Port.Error`) or a socket failure
        into a `Debugger.Error` with reason `Disconnected`. The debuggee is killed in a
        `finally`, so it never outlives the block.
  entry .sessional   the given which makes `debuggee.session { … }` resolve, from the launch givens in scope
      In the companion, so it is found without an import; it needs the same contextual values as
      `Debuggee.Sessional` to be in scope at the call.

entry .Debugger   an attach target: a JVM already listening for a JDWP connection, by its endpoint
  detail
      `Debugger(endpoint)` wraps anything `Connectable` and `Showable` — typically an
      `Endpoint[Tcp.Port]` — and `target.session { … }` connects to it, exchanges the handshake,
      negotiates identifier sizes and lends a `Debug` for the block, closing the connection
      afterwards. The VM must already be listening (started with `-agentlib:jdwp=…,server=y`),
      and whoever started it owns its standard streams: an attach session has no
      `Debug.Console`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads

      def attached(): List[ThreadId] = supervise:
        Debugger(Endpoint(t"localhost", Port[Tcp](5005))).session:
          debug.threads()
      ```

      The companion also holds `Debugger.Error`, the one error type every session operation
      raises.
  refer vivisection.Debuggee
  refer vivisection.Debug
  entry #endpoint   the connectable endpoint the session dials
  entry .apply   `Debugger(endpoint)`: an attach target over any `Connectable` endpoint
  entry .Sessional   the `aperture.Sessional` which attaches to an endpoint and lends a `Debug`
    detail
        Generic over the endpoint type, with the socket `Connectable` resolved at the call site
        through the context bound. It takes a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]`,
        a `Tactic[Socket.Error]` (raised when the connection cannot be made), a
        `Socket.Event is Loggable`, a `Tactic[Debugger.Error]` and `Diagnostics`.
    entry #session   dials the endpoint, handshakes, and runs the block with a `Debug` lent to it
  entry .sessional   the given which makes `debugger.session { … }` resolve, for any connectable endpoint
      In the companion; needs the same contextual values as `Debugger.Sessional` at the call.
  entry .Error   raised when a JDWP command fails, the connection is lost, or a launch does not come up
    detail
        Error 601, with the reason's own JDWP error number as its sub-number: _the JDWP action
        failed because `reason`: `detail`_. Every operation on `Debug`, `Halt` and
        `Jdwp.Connection` takes a `Tactic[Debugger.Error]`; the VM's error code in a reply is
        decoded to a `Debugger.Error.Reason` and raised with the command's set and number as the
        detail, and a lost connection fails every in-flight request with `Disconnected`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        def describe(error: Debugger.Error): Text = error.reason match
          case Debugger.Error.Reason.Disconnected => t"the debuggee went away: ${error.detail}"
          case Debugger.Error.Reason.VmDead       => t"the VM has exited"
          case other                              => t"JDWP error ${other.number}"
        ```

        A handler runs on the dispatcher task, and the `Halt` it is lent carries the tactic the
        dispatcher supplied, so a handler needs no error capability of its own; a failure inside
        a handler is swallowed by the dispatcher rather than propagated to the session's thread.
    entry #reason   why the action failed, as a `Debugger.Error.Reason`
    entry #detail   which command or step failed, as text
    entry .Reason   the JDWP error codes, by their published names, plus `Disconnected` and `Other`
      detail
          Each case carries its JDWP `number`: `InvalidThread` (10), `InvalidThreadGroup` (11),
          `ThreadNotSuspended` (13), `ThreadSuspended` (14), `InvalidObject` (20),
          `InvalidClass` (21), `ClassNotPrepared` (22), `InvalidMethod` (23),
          `InvalidLocation` (24), `InvalidField` (25), `InvalidFrame` (30), `NoMoreFrames`
          (31), `OpaqueFrame` (32), `TypeMismatch` (34), `InvalidSlot` (35), `NotFound` (41),
          `NotImplemented` (99), `NullPointer` (100), `AbsentInformation` (101),
          `InvalidEventType` (102), `IllegalArgument` (103), `VmDead` (112), `Internal` (113)
          and `AlreadyInvoking` (502). `Disconnected` (−1) originates in this library, not the
          VM, for a lost connection or a launch which never came up; `Other(code)` keeps any
          number the VM sent which is not named here, so decoding never loses it.
      entry #number   the JDWP error code, or −1 for `Disconnected`
      entry .apply   `Reason(code)`: the reason for a JDWP error code, `Other(code)` for an unrecognised one
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message, _the thread was not suspended_ and the like

entry .Debug   a live debug session: the capability lent inside `target.session { … }`
  detail
      `Debug` is the programmer-facing surface over a `Jdwp.Connection`: it lists, names,
      suspends and resumes threads, reads their frames, sets breakpoints and watchpoints,
      requests steps, and exposes the VM's events as a stream. It is an
      `ExclusiveCapability`, lent to the session block and confined there by capture checking,
      and is reached from context with the `debug` accessor rather than named.

      Every operation takes a `Tactic[Debugger.Error]`. The session runs its reader, writer and
      dispatcher as tasks, so it lives inside `supervise`; the handler given to a breakpoint,
      step, exception request or watchpoint runs on the dispatcher task with a `Halt` lent for
      the stop, and the stopped thread resumes when the handler returns unless the handler
      calls `remain()`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.{Variable as _, *}
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      def classpath: Text = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Debug]).apply()
      val target: Debuggee = Debuggee(sh"java -classpath $classpath com.example.Main", port = 0)
      ```

      A session, watched one stage at a time:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Launch the debuggee, suspended at startup
        code
            supervise:
              target.session:
                debug.threads().map(debug.name(_))
      frame
        label Set a breakpoint by source line before the class is even loaded
        code
            supervise:
              target.session:
                val stopped: Promise[ThreadId] = Promise()

                debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(12)): stop ?=>
                  stopped.offer(stop.thread)
                  stop.remain()
      frame
        label Resume, and wait for the hit
        code
            supervise:
              target.session:
                val stopped: Promise[ThreadId] = Promise()

                debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(12)): stop ?=>
                  stopped.offer(stop.thread)
                  stop.remain()

                debug.resume()
                val thread: ThreadId = stopped.await()
      frame
        label Read the stopped frame's variables inside the handler
        code
            supervise:
              target.session:
                val stopped: Promise[ThreadId] = Promise()
                val seen: Promise[List[Text]] = Promise()

                debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(12)): stop ?=>
                  stopped.offer(stop.thread)
                  seen.offer(stop.variables().map(_.inspect))
                  stop.remain()

                debug.resume()
                val thread: ThreadId = stopped.await()
                seen.await()   // List(t"total:42", t"tag:\"answer\"", …)
      frame
        label Step over the line, and see where the thread lands
        code
            supervise:
              target.session:
                val stopped: Promise[ThreadId] = Promise()
                val seen: Promise[List[Text]] = Promise()
                val landed: Promise[(Text, Int)] = Promise()

                debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(12)): stop ?=>
                  stopped.offer(stop.thread)
                  seen.offer(stop.variables().map(_.inspect))
                  stop.remain()

                debug.resume()
                val thread: ThreadId = stopped.await()
                seen.await()

                debug.step(thread, Jdwp.StepDepth.Over): step ?=>
                  landed.offer(step.describe(step.location))
                  step.remain()

                debug.resume()
                landed.await()   // (t"com.example.Main.run", 13)
      ##
      ```

      The VM suspends once per *composite* of events, and the dispatcher resumes it exactly
      once by the composite's suspend policy, after every handler for it has run — unless one
      asked to `remain()`, or no handler claimed any of its events, in which case the composite
      is handed to `#events` and its consumer owns the resumption. A thread held by `remain()`
      is released with `#resume`.
  refer vivisection.Halt
  refer vivisection.Debuggee
  refer vivisection.Debugger
  refer vivisection.debug
  entry #console   the launched debuggee's output and exit status, or `Unset` for an attached one
      A `Debug.Console` is present only in a `Debuggee` session: an attached VM's streams
      belong to whoever started it.
  entry #capabilities   the VM's advertised capabilities, fetched once and memoized
      `VirtualMachine.CapabilitiesNew`, as a `Jdwp.Capabilities`. A frontend forwards several
      of its flags when announcing what it supports, and the session itself consults
      `canUseSourceNameFilters`, `canPopFrames`, `canWatchFieldAccess`,
      `canWatchFieldModification`, `canGetBytecodes` and `canGetConstantPool`.
  entry #version   the VM's version, as a `Jdwp.Version`
  entry #threads   every thread in the VM, as `ThreadId`s
  entry #suspend   suspends every thread, or `suspend(thread)` one thread
      JDWP counts suspensions: a thread suspended twice needs two resumes. The session's own
      stops are settled by the dispatcher; `suspend` is for the caller's own pauses.
  entry #resume   resumes every thread, or `resume(thread)` one thread
      After a handler has called `remain()`, this is what lets the program continue; the DAP
      adapter's `continue` is exactly `debug.resume()`.
  entry #name   a thread's name, as the VM reports it
  entry #frames   a thread's call stack, innermost first, as `(FrameId, Jdwp.Location)` pairs
      The thread must be suspended: a running thread's frames are `InvalidThread` or
      `ThreadNotSuspended`. A frame identifier is valid only until the thread resumes.
  entry #events   the stream of composite events no registered handler claimed
      The primitive beneath the handlers: every `Jdwp.Event.Composite` the VM sends which no
      breakpoint, step, exception, watch or prepare handler claimed. A caller drains it and
      reacts, or awaits a particular event, and owns any resumption the composite's suspend
      policy calls for. Reading consumes the events as they arrive, and the stream ends when
      the connection closes.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      def trace()(using Debug^, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Unit =
        debug.events.each: composite =>
          composite.events.each:
            case Jdwp.Event.Breakpoint(request, thread, location) => debug.resume()
            case Jdwp.Event.VmDeath(_)                            => ()
            case other                                            => ()
      ```
  entry #breakpoint   sets a breakpoint: by resolved location, by source file and line, or by class and method
    detail
        Four forms, each returning what revokes the breakpoint — a request id or a handle:

        - `breakpoint(location, policy)` sets a raw breakpoint at a `Jdwp.Location` with a
          `Jdwp.SuspendPolicy` (`All` by default) and returns the request id, used with
          `Debug#clear`. Each hit arrives on `Debug#events` as a `Jdwp.Event.Breakpoint`, suspending as
          the policy says, and the caller resumes.
        - `breakpoint(location)(handler)` sets one at a resolved location whose hits run
          `handler` on the dispatcher, returning a `Breakpoint`.
        - `breakpoint(source, line)(handler)` and `breakpoint(source, line, bound)(handler)`
          set one by source file name and line: it binds now in every loaded class the
          position resolves in, and later in each matching class as it is prepared, so it can be
          placed before the class is loaded — the launch order a frontend uses. Binding on
          prepare happens while the loading thread stands suspended, strictly before execution
          can reach the line, and inlined copies of the position are found through the class's
          SMAP, so a breakpoint on inlined code hits where the code was written. `bound` is
          called at each successful binding, which is how a frontend learns that an unverified
          breakpoint has become real. Returns a `SourceBreakpoint`.
        - `breakpoint(className, method)(handler)` and the `bound` form set one at the entry of
          every method of that name on the named *runtime* class (an `object`'s methods live on
          its `$`-suffixed class), every overload included, binding now or on preparation as
          the source form does. Methods with no executable line information (abstract, native)
          are skipped.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.{Variable as _, *}

        def watchpoints()(using Debug^, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Unit =
          val byLine: SourceBreakpoint^ = debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(42)): stop ?=>
            stop.variables().each: variable =>
              Out.println(variable.inspect)

          val byMethod: SourceBreakpoint^ = debug.breakpoint(t"com.example.Ledger", t"deposit"): stop ?=>
            stop.remain()

          byLine.clear()
        ```

        A conditional breakpoint is simply a handler which tests its condition and does nothing
        otherwise. The handler runs with the thread suspended under the `All` policy, and the
        dispatcher resumes afterwards unless the handler calls `remain()`.
  entry #classPrepare   asks to be told when a class matching a pattern is prepared
      Sets a `ClassPrepare` request for the JDWP class-match `pattern` (an exact name, or one
      bounded by a single `*`), returning its request id. Each match arrives on `Debug#events` as a
      `Jdwp.Event.ClassPrepared`, suspending the loading thread, so a caller can resolve a
      breakpoint in a class not yet loaded when the session began — with `Debug#locateIn` against
      the reported type, never `Debug#locate`, since a whole-VM scan can deadlock against the lock
      the suspended thread holds.
  entry #step   requests a step on a suspended thread: a logical step with a handler, or a raw one
    detail
        `step(thread, depth)(handler)` requests a *logical* step whose completion runs
        `handler` on the dispatcher exactly as a breakpoint hit does, suspending only the
        stepped thread. The thread must be suspended when the step is requested — typically it
        is the thread a handler was just lent, held with `remain()` — and the request is spent
        on the resumption which follows:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*

        def over(thread: ThreadId)(using Debug^, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Unit =
          debug.step(thread, Jdwp.StepDepth.Over): stop ?=>
            Out.println(stop.describe(stop.location)(0))

          debug.resume()
        ```

        A logical step completes when the *source position* changes, read through the class's
        SMAP: stepping over a line containing an inline call passes over the whole line, body
        and all, where the bytecode would descend into the inlined body; stepping into one
        stops at the first line of the inlined body, as if it were a call; and a landing in
        plumbing — a bridge, a mixin or static forwarder, a field or lazy-val accessor, a
        synthetic method other than a lambda body, or any class under `java.*`, `javax.*`,
        `jdk.*`, `sun.*`, `com.sun.*` or `scala.runtime.*` — is never shown: the step continues
        inward for `Into` and out otherwise, until it rests in code the programmer wrote.
        Accessors and forwarders carry no flag, so they are recognised by their bytecode shape,
        which needs the VM's `canGetBytecodes` and `canGetConstantPool`. A landing the SMAP
        cannot read, or the 64-iteration safety cap, reports the stop as it is.

        `step(thread, depth, size, excluding)` is the raw request: it sets a single-step
        request with a `Jdwp.StepSize` (`Line` by default) and `ClassExclude` modifiers for
        each pattern in `excluding`, returning the request id. The VM reports the landing as a
        `Jdwp.Event.SingleStep` on `Debug#events`, wherever it is — inside the JDK, a forwarder or
        an inlined body — and the caller resumes.
  entry #pause   suspends a thread on the caller's initiative and hands back a `Halt` over its top frame
      No event marks the stop, but the halt offers the same view of the thread — frames,
      variables, assignment — as a breakpoint would, which is how a client-driven pause is
      served. The caller owns the suspension: dropping the halt does not resume the thread;
      `Debug#resume` does.
  entry #clear   cancels an event request by its kind and request id
      `clear(Jdwp.EventKind.Breakpoint, request)` removes the request a raw `Debug#breakpoint`,
      `Debug#classPrepare` or raw `Debug#step` returned. A handler-bearing request is revoked through its
      `Breakpoint` or `SourceBreakpoint` handle instead, which also unregisters the handler.
  entry #exceptions   stops when an exception is thrown — uncaught, caught, or both — within matching classes
      `exceptions(uncaught = true, caught = false, within = Unset)(handler)` sets an exception
      request and runs `handler` at each throw with a `Halt` whose `cause` is
      `Halt.Cause.Thrown`, so `exceptionInfo()` describes the exception in flight. `within`
      restricts the request to exceptions thrown from classes matching a class pattern: a
      caught-exception request without one stops inside the platform's own exception-driven
      control flow, which starts long before any application class runs. Returns a
      `Breakpoint` which revokes the request.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      def uncaught()(using Debug^, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Breakpoint^ =
        debug.exceptions(uncaught = true, caught = true, within = t"com.example.*"): stop ?=>
          stop.exceptionInfo().let: info =>
            Out.println(t"${info.className}: ${info.message.or(t"")}")
      ```
  entry #watch   stops when a field of a loaded class is written, or with `access`, read
      `watch(className, field, access = false)(handler)` finds the field on the named runtime
      class — which must already be loaded, as it always is when the watch is placed from a
      live variables view — and runs `handler` at each write with a `Halt` whose `cause` is
      `Halt.Cause.Modification`, carrying the incoming value observed *before* the write lands
      (or `Halt.Cause.Access` for a read). The result is `Unset` when the class or field cannot
      be found, or the VM lacks `canWatchFieldModification` (or `canWatchFieldAccess`).

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      def balance()(using Debug^, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Optional[Breakpoint^] =
        debug.watch(t"com.example.Ledger$$Account", t"balance"): stop ?=>
          stop.cause match
            case Halt.Cause.Modification(_, _, _, incoming) => Out.println(incoming.inspect)
            case _                                          => ()
      ```
  entry #logpoint   a breakpoint which logs a message and immediately resumes
      `logpoint(location)(message)` installs a breakpoint whose handler logs
      `Debug.Event.Logpoint(message)` at `info` level and returns, so the program is never
      paused beyond the handler itself. `message` is evaluated with the `Halt` in scope, so it
      can read the stopped frame's variables. Needs a `Debug.Event is Loggable` in scope as well
      as the tactic.
  entry #locate   the executable locations a source line currently resolves to, across loaded classes
      Every method of every loaded class compiled from `source` whose line table covers `line`,
      inlined copies found through each class's SMAP included. Classes named after the file are
      tried first, so the common case costs a handful of round trips; a sweep of the remaining
      loaded classes — skipping array types and the platform's own — backstops it. Classes not
      yet loaded are not found: the source form of `Debug#breakpoint` defers to their preparation.
      Not safe while a thread is suspended at a `ClassPrepare` event: use `Debug#locateIn`.
  entry #locateIn   the executable locations a source line resolves to within one reference type
      As `Debug#locate`, for the one class `cls` (with its `Jdwp.TypeTag`): every method compiled
      from `source` whose line table covers `line`, plus any inlined copies of the position its
      SMAP records, de-duplicated by method and bytecode index. It never enumerates the VM's
      classes, so it is safe to call from a `ClassPrepared` event's handler, where a whole-VM
      scan can deadlock against the class-loading lock the suspended thread holds.
  entry .Handler   the type of a breakpoint or step handler: `(halt: Halt^) ?=> Unit`
      A context function over a lent `Halt`, run on the dispatcher task when a hit is claimed.
      The halt carries the tactic the dispatcher supplied, so a handler needs no error
      capability of its own; it is written `stop ?=> …`, since the natural name `halt` belongs
      to fulminate.
  entry .Console   a launched debuggee's standard output, standard error and exit status
    detail
        Present at `Debug#console` in a `Debuggee` session only. The child's output and error
        pipes are drained from the moment of the fork into two windows a reader consumes as
        streams, and its exit status settles a promise. The DAP adapter relays the streams as
        `output` events and the exit as `exited` and `terminated`.
    entry #stdout   the debuggee's standard output, as a stream of chunks
    entry #stderr   the debuggee's standard error, as a stream of chunks
    entry #exited   a promise of the debuggee's exit status, settled when the process ends
  entry .Event   what a session logs on its own initiative: a logpoint's message
    detail
        One case, `Logpoint(message)`, tagged `Log.Runtime` and logged at `info` by
        `Debug#logpoint`. A `Debug.Event is Loggable` must be in scope to install a logpoint;
        `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
    entry .communicable   renders a logpoint event as its message

entry .debug   the ambient debug session, summoned from context inside a session block
    `debug` names the `Debug` capability a `session` block was lent, without naming the
    parameter: `target.session { debug.threads() }`, in the manner of `monitor` for a
    supervision scope. It is a `transparent inline def` resolving to the contextual `Debug^`,
    so it carries that capability's exact capture set. No matching accessor exists for `Halt`
    — its natural name is taken by `fulminate.halt` — so handlers name their parameter,
    conventionally `stop ?=>`.

entry .Breakpoint   a revocable handle on an installed breakpoint, exception request or watchpoint
  detail
      Returned by `Debug#breakpoint(location)(handler)`, `Debug#exceptions` and
      `Debug#watch`. It captures the session it belongs to, so it cannot outlive the session
      block. `#clear` removes the request from the VM and unregisters its handler, after which
      any hits already in flight are treated as unclaimed and reach `Debug#events`.
  refer vivisection.SourceBreakpoint
  entry #request   the JDWP request id of the installed event request
  entry #clear   removes the request from the VM and unregisters its handler

entry .SourceBreakpoint   a revocable handle on a breakpoint set by source position or by class and method
  detail
      Returned by the source-line and class-and-method forms of `Debug#breakpoint`. A
      source breakpoint binds to an executable location in each matching class as it is loaded,
      so its `#locations` grow over the session; a frontend reads an empty list as an
      unverified breakpoint and a later binding as its verification (the `bound` callback
      reports each). `#clear` removes every bound breakpoint and revokes the class-prepare
      request which would bind more. The handle captures the session, so it cannot escape the
      session block.
  refer vivisection.Breakpoint
  entry #locations   the executable locations this breakpoint has bound to so far
      Empty while every class the position resolves in remains unloaded.
  entry #bound   whether at least one location has been bound
  entry #clear   removes every bound breakpoint and the prepare request which binds more

entry .Halt   a stopped thread, as the capability lent to a handler while the thread stands suspended
  detail
      A `Halt` is the view a breakpoint, step, exception or watch handler is lent: the thread
      which stopped, the location it stopped at, why (`#cause`), its frames and their logical
      variables, the exception in flight if that is why it stopped, and the means to assign a
      variable, pop a frame, or hold the thread suspended. It is an `ExclusiveCapability`, so
      it cannot outlive the stop it describes: once the dispatcher resumes the thread, every
      identifier the halt handed out — frames, objects, slots — may be stale.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.{Variable as _, *}

      def inspect()(using Debug^, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Unit =
        debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(42)): stop ?=>
          stop.frames().each: (frame, location) =>
            val (name, line) = stop.describe(location)
            Out.println(t"$name:$line")

          stop.variables().each: variable =>
            Out.println(t"${variable.name}: ${variable.erased} = ${variable.value.inspect}")

          stop.remain()
      ```

      The halt carries the `Tactic[Debugger.Error]` the dispatcher supplied when it minted it,
      so its operations take none: a handler runs on the dispatcher task, and the error channel
      must unwind that stack, not the registration site's. Handlers are written `stop ?=> …`:
      the accessor name `halt` belongs to fulminate.
  refer vivisection.Debug
  refer vivisection.Variable
  refer vivisection.evaluator
  entry #thread   the thread which stopped
  entry #location   where it stopped: the class, method and bytecode index
  entry #cause   why it stopped, as a `Halt.Cause`
  entry #remain   asks the dispatcher not to resume the thread when the handlers for this stop return
      The caller then owns the suspension and resumes it — or not — through `Debug#resume`.
      Shared by every handler run for one composite, since JDWP suspends once per composite and
      the dispatcher settles that suspension once.
  entry #frames   the stopped thread's call stack, innermost first, as `(FrameId, Jdwp.Location)` pairs
  entry #positions   the logical source positions a frame stands for, innermost first
      One `Halt.Position` per inline origin recovered from the class's SMAP — the file and line
      where the inlined code was written, marked `inlined` — followed by the physical frame
      itself at its real, call-site line. A frame with no SMAP, one stopped at a real line, or a
      VM without `canGetSourceDebugExtension` yields exactly the single position `Halt#describe`
      reports. This is how a stack trace shows an inlined body as a frame of its own.
  entry #describe   a readable name and line for a location: `pkg.Cls.method` and its source line
      Resolved from the class's method and line tables; the line is `0` where no line
      information exists. The name is demangled as a stack trace renders it: a module class's
      trailing `$` dropped, inner-class `$`s read as dots.
  entry #exceptionInfo   the exception in flight at an exception stop, or `Unset` at any other
      A `Halt.ExceptionInfo` when `Halt#cause` is `Thrown`: the exception's class name, the detail
      message it was constructed with (`Unset` when null), and whether the throw will be
      caught. The message is read straight from `Throwable`'s `detailMessage` field, walking up
      the hierarchy to the class which declares it, rather than by invoking `getMessage`, so no
      debuggee code runs.
  entry #thisObject   a frame's `this`, or `Unset` in a static or native frame
  entry #variables   the logical variables visible at the top frame, or at a given frame
      `variables()` reads the frame where the thread stopped; `variables(frame, location)` any
      frame of its stack. The result is the live local slots at the location — a slot is live
      only within its bytecode range — followed by the state captured on `this` and its
      `$outer` chain, each recovered in the form the programmer wrote: a synthetic `name$3`
      capture field surfaces as `name`, an outer accessor's `pkg$Owner$$seed` as `seed`, a
      captured `var`'s ref cell is unboxed to its `elem` (and the variable marked mutable), and
      a lazy val's backing field is read but never forced, so an unevaluated one is reported
      `Unforced` with no value. Ordinary member fields appear against their owner. A method
      compiled without a variable table degrades to the captured and field state alone.
  entry #children   expands a snapshot one level: an object's instance fields, or an array's elements
      For a `Variable.Snapshot.Obj`, the object's non-static fields as variables; for an
      `Arr`, its first 256 elements, named by their zero-based index. Anything else expands to
      nothing. This is the `variables` request behind a frontend's tree view.
  entry #pop   discards a frame and every frame above it, so resuming re-executes the call which made it
      A frontend's frame restart. Requires the VM's `canPopFrames` capability and no native
      frame among those popped.
  entry #assign   writes a value into a variable at the stopped frame, routed by its provenance
      `assign(variable, value)` writes at the top frame; `assign(frame, variable, value)` at a
      given one. The write goes where the variable's `Variable.Provenance` says its storage is:
      a local slot in the frame, a captured or member field on its holder, an array element,
      or — through a ref cell — the cell's `elem`, so assigning a captured `var` behaves as the
      source suggests. The value must be of the variable's erased type (a `Jdwp.Value.OfInt`
      for an `Int`); the identifiers a provenance carries are valid only while the thread
      stands suspended, which is exactly when assignment is meaningful. `Evaluator#assign`
      evaluates an expression at the variable's declared type and assigns the result.
  entry .Cause   why a thread stopped: a plain stop, a thrown exception, or a field access or modification
    detail
        `Stopped` covers breakpoints, steps, pauses and method entry and exit, where the handler
        already knows which request it registered. `Thrown(exception, catchLocation)` carries
        the exception object and where it will be caught, `Unset` for an uncaught one.
        `Access(cls, field, target)` and `Modification(cls, field, target, incoming)` carry the
        class and field touched and the object touched (`Unset` for a static field); a
        modification also carries the `incoming` value, observed before the write lands.
  entry .ExceptionInfo   a summary of an exception in flight: its class, its message, and whether it is caught
    entry #className   the exception's class, demangled as a stack trace renders it
    entry #message   the detail message it was constructed with, or `Unset` when null
    entry #caught   whether the throw will be caught
  entry .Position   one logical source position a physical frame stands for
    detail
        For inlined code, the file and line where the code was written; for the frame itself,
        its own source and its real line. `name` is the display name (the class or method);
        `source` the file's recorded basename and `path` its fuller path, when known; `inlined`
        whether this is an inline origin rather than the frame; and `cls` the raw
        `ScalaClass`-stratum class name, for a caller holding the classpath to resolve the
        definition through TASTy, as `Namer` does.
    entry #name   the display name: a class and method, or the inline origin's class
    entry #source   the source file's basename, if known
    entry #path   the source file's fuller path, if the SMAP records one
    entry #line   the source line
    entry #inlined   whether this position is an inline origin rather than the physical frame
    entry #cls   the raw class name the SMAP records for an inline origin, if any

entry .Variable   a logical variable at a suspended frame, in the form the programmer wrote it
  detail
      A `Variable` is what `Halt#variables` recovers: its `name` as written (captures
      un-flattened from their synthetic fields), its `erased` type from the JVM signature, its
      `static` type when `Evaluator#variables` has recovered the declared one from TASTy, its
      `value` as a `Variable.Snapshot` (absent for an unforced lazy val), the `provenance`
      which says where its storage is so a write knows where to go, whether it is `mutable`
      (a `var`, boxed in a ref cell), and its `state` — `Forced` or, for a lazy val not yet
      evaluated, `Unforced`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.{Variable as _, *}

      def render(variable: vivisection.Variable): Text =
        variable.state match
          case vivisection.Variable.State.Unforced => t"${variable.name}: ∿∿∿"
          case vivisection.Variable.State.Forced   =>
            t"${variable.name}: ${variable.static.or(variable.erased)} = ${variable.value.inspect}"
      ```

      `Variable` is not exported to `soundness`, which already has ambience's `Variable` (an
      environment variable): write `vivisection.Variable`, or `import soundness.{Variable as _,
      *}` and then `import vivisection.Variable`.
  refer vivisection.Halt
  refer vivisection.Evaluator
  entry #name   the name as written in the source
  entry #static   the declared Scala type, when recovered from TASTy by an `Evaluator`; `Unset` otherwise
  entry #erased   the JVM type, demangled: `Int`, `Array[Int]`, `scala.collection.List`
  entry #value   the value, as a `Variable.Snapshot`; `Unset` for an unforced lazy val
  entry #provenance   where the storage is, so an assignment knows where to write
  entry #mutable   whether the variable is a `var`: its storage is a `scala.runtime.*Ref` cell
  entry #state   whether a lazy binding has been evaluated: `Forced`, or `Unforced`
  entry .inspectable   renders a variable as `name:value`, with `∿∿∿` for an unforced lazy val and `○` for no value
  entry .Snapshot   the debugger-side view of a remote value: a primitive, a string, an array prefix, an object, or null
    detail
        `Primitive(value)` holds a decoded `Jdwp.Value`; `Str(id, text)` a string, fetched
        eagerly; `Arr(id, component, length, prefix)` an array's identity, component `Jdwp.Tag`,
        length and the first ten elements, fetched eagerly; `Obj(id, cls)` any other object as
        a summary — its identity and demangled class — for `Halt#children` to expand; and
        `Null` the null reference.
    entry .inspectable   renders a snapshot: literals as themselves, arrays as `⦋🅸₀1∣₁2∣…⦌＠id`, objects as `Cls＠id`
        A remote identity renders as the class name (or the array's component letter) followed
        by a fullwidth `＠` and the JDWP object identifier — close enough to Java's `Foo@1a2b`
        to read as an identity, and distinct from any literal rendering. The given is
        parameterised over the enumeration's case types, since `Inspectable` is invariant.
  entry .Provenance   where a variable's storage was found, carrying whatever identity a write needs
    detail
        `Local(slot)` is a frame slot; `Captured(fieldPath, holder, field)` a capture field on
        `this` or an `$outer` along `fieldPath`; `Field(owner, target, field)` a member field on
        `target`, with its demangled `owner` class; `Element(array, index)` an array element;
        and `Cell(cell, elem, inner)` the `elem` field of a `scala.runtime.*Ref` cell found at
        `inner`. The identifiers are stable only while the halt's thread stands suspended.
  entry .State   whether a lazy binding has been evaluated: `Forced` or `Unforced`
      An unforced lazy val is *never* forced by the debugger — forcing would change the program
      under observation — so its value is absent and it renders as `∿∿∿`.

entry .ObjectId   a JDWP object identifier: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Object]`
    Every identifier the VM hands out is a `Jdwp.Ref` tagged by a phantom kind, so the nine
    aliases are distinct at compile time and free at runtime. `ThreadId`, `ThreadGroupId`,
    `StringId` and `ClassLoaderId` widen to `ObjectId`, since in JDWP a thread, a thread group,
    a string and a class loader all *are* objects; `ReferenceTypeId`, `MethodId`, `FieldId` and
    `FrameId` do not. The null object is `Jdwp.Ref.empty`, tested with the `empty` extension.
entry .ThreadId   a thread's identifier: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Thread]`, which widens to `ObjectId`
entry .ThreadGroupId   a thread group's identifier: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Group]`, which widens to `ObjectId`
entry .StringId   a string object's identifier: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Str]`, which widens to `ObjectId`
entry .ClassLoaderId   a class loader's identifier: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Loader]`, which widens to `ObjectId`
entry .ReferenceTypeId   a class, interface or array type's identifier: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Type]`
entry .MethodId   a method's identifier, scoped to its reference type: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Method]`
entry .FieldId   a field's identifier, scoped to its reference type: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Field]`
entry .FrameId   a stack frame's identifier, valid while its thread is suspended: `Jdwp.Ref[Jdwp.Ref.Frame]`

entry .Jdwp   the Java Debug Wire Protocol as values: identifiers, locations, values, events, codecs and a connection
  detail
      `Jdwp` models the protocol so that this library can be read against the specification:
      command sets and commands keep their published numbers, event and error kinds their
      published names, and identifiers the widths the VM negotiates. It is the layer beneath
      `Debug`, for work the session does not expose: a raw event request with its own
      `Jdwp.Modifier`s, a method invoked in the debuggee, a string interned there.

      Its pieces are the opaque `Ref` (with the identifier aliases `ThreadId`, `FrameId` and
      the rest built on it), the enumerations a request or reply carries (`TypeTag`,
      `SuspendPolicy`, `EventKind`, `StepDepth`, `StepSize`, `Tag`), `Location`, the tagged
      `Value`, the `Modifier`s which constrain an event request, the decoded reply structures
      (`Version`, `ClassInfo`, `MethodInfo`, `FieldInfo`, `LineTable`, `VariableTable`,
      `Capabilities`, …), the `Event`s a VM sends back, the hand-written big-endian codecs
      `Reader` and `Writer`, the `Packet` framing, and `Connection`, the wire-level client which
      frames and correlates packets over a duplex channel. Nothing but `Connection` touches a
      socket.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val sizes: Jdwp.IdSizes = Jdwp.IdSizes(8, 8, 8, 8, 8)
      val writer: Jdwp.Writer = Jdwp.Writer(sizes)
      writer.byte(2).int(1).threadId(Jdwp.Ref(7L)).location(Jdwp.Location(Jdwp.TypeTag.Class, Jdwp.Ref(3L), Jdwp.Ref(4L), 0L))
      val reader: Jdwp.Reader^ = Jdwp.Reader(writer.data, sizes)
      reader.byte()
      ```

      Requests and replies are correlated by packet id, so many commands may be in flight at
      once and an event arriving during one does not disturb it.
  refer vivisection.Debug
  refer vivisection.Halt
  entry .Handshake   the fourteen ASCII bytes `JDWP-Handshake` each end sends, and expects back, before any packet
  entry .Ref   one opaque `Long` tagged by a phantom kind: the type beneath every JDWP identifier
    detail
        `Ref[kind]` is a `Long` at runtime and a distinct type per `Ref.Kind` at compile time,
        so a `ThreadId` can never be passed where a `FieldId` is wanted, while the covariance
        in the kind lets a `ThreadId` widen to an `ObjectId`. `Ref(long)` makes one and
        `Ref.empty` is the null object (identifier zero); the extensions `long` and `empty` read
        one back. The wire width of each kind is negotiated per connection (`IdSizes`), which is
        why `Reader` and `Writer` are built with the sizes.
    entry .Kind   the root of the phantom kinds a reference may carry
    entry .Object   the kind of any object reference; `Thread`, `Group`, `Str` and `Loader` extend it
    entry .Thread   the kind of a thread reference
    entry .Group   the kind of a thread-group reference
    entry .Str   the kind of a string reference
    entry .Loader   the kind of a class-loader reference
    entry .Type   the kind of a reference-type (class, interface or array type) identifier
    entry .Method   the kind of a method identifier
    entry .Field   the kind of a field identifier
    entry .Frame   the kind of a frame identifier
    entry .apply   `Ref[kind](long)`: wraps an identifier the VM sent
    entry .empty   the null object, identifier zero, at any kind
    entry .showable   renders a reference as its decimal identifier
    entry .equality   `CanEqual` between references of the same kind
  entry .long   the identifier a reference wraps, as a `Long`; an extension on any `Ref`
  entry .empty   whether a reference is the null object (identifier zero); an extension on any `Ref`
  entry .IdSizes   the five negotiated identifier widths, in bytes, each 1 to 8
    detail
        `VirtualMachine.IDSizes`: `field`, `method`, `objectId` (which also sizes strings,
        threads, thread groups, class loaders, class objects and arrays), `referenceType` and
        `frame`. A `Reader` or `Writer` is built with the sizes the connection negotiated
        (`Connection#sizes`); `IdSizes.bootstrap` is all-eights, used only to decode the
        fixed-layout IDSizes reply itself and to frame packets, which carry no identifiers.
    entry #field   the width of a field identifier
    entry #method   the width of a method identifier
    entry #objectId   the width of an object identifier, and of every kind which widens to one
    entry #referenceType   the width of a reference-type identifier
    entry #frame   the width of a frame identifier
    entry .bootstrap   all-eights: the sizes used before negotiation, for the IDSizes reply and packet headers
  entry .TypeTag   whether a reference type is a class (1), an interface (2) or an array (3)
    entry .apply   `TypeTag(byte)`: the tag for a wire byte, `Class` for an unrecognised one
    entry #id   the wire byte
  entry .SuspendPolicy   how much of the VM to suspend when an event fires: `None`, `EventThread` or `All`
    detail
        `None` (0) suspends nothing, `EventThread` (1) only the thread which raised the event,
        `All` (2) every thread. JDWP suspends once per composite, and the dispatcher resumes by
        the composite's policy; a handler-bearing breakpoint uses `All`, a step or class-prepare
        request `EventThread`.
    entry .apply   `SuspendPolicy(byte)`: the policy for a wire byte, `None` for an unrecognised one
    entry #id   the wire byte
  entry .EventKind   the kinds of event a request may ask for and a composite may report, by their JDWP numbers
    detail
        `SingleStep` (1), `Breakpoint` (2), `FramePop` (3), `Exception` (4), `UserDefined` (5),
        `ThreadStart` (6), `ThreadDeath` (7), `ClassPrepare` (8), `ClassUnload` (9),
        `ClassLoad` (10), `FieldAccess` (20), `FieldModified` (21), `MethodEntry` (40),
        `MethodExit` (41), `VmStart` (90), `VmDeath` (99), and `Other(id)` for any number not
        named here. `Debug#clear` takes the kind of the request to cancel.
    entry .apply   `EventKind(int)`: the kind for a wire number, `Other(id)` for an unrecognised one
    entry #id   the wire number
  entry .StepDepth   the direction of a step: `Into` (0), `Over` (1) or `Out` (2)
    entry #id   the wire number
  entry .StepSize   the grain of a step: `Min` (0), one bytecode instruction, or `Line` (1), one source line
    entry #id   the wire number
  entry .Tag   the tag byte identifying the runtime type of a value on the wire
    detail
        `ArrayTag` (`[`), `ByteTag` (`B`), `CharTag` (`C`), `ObjectTag` (`L`), `FloatTag`
        (`F`), `DoubleTag` (`D`), `IntTag` (`I`), `LongTag` (`J`), `ShortTag` (`S`), `VoidTag`
        (`V`), `BooleanTag` (`Z`), `StringTag` (`s`), `ThreadTag` (`t`), `GroupTag` (`g`),
        `LoaderTag` (`l`) and `ClassTag0` (`c`, named so as not to shadow `scala.reflect.ClassTag`).
        A value is requested with the tag of its declared type — the first character of a JVM
        signature — and the reply carries the precise tag.
    entry .apply   `Tag(char)`: the tag for a signature or wire character, `ObjectTag` for an unrecognised one
    entry .isObject   whether a tag's wire payload is an object identifier rather than an inline primitive
    entry #id   the wire character
  entry .Location   an executable location: a reference type, one of its methods, and a bytecode index within it
    entry #tag   whether the type is a class, interface or array
    entry #cls   the reference type
    entry #method   the method
    entry #index   the bytecode index within the method; a line table maps it to a source line
  entry .Value   a tagged value read from or written to a slot, field or array element
    detail
        Primitives are decoded eagerly as `OfByte`, `OfChar`, `OfDouble`, `OfFloat`, `OfInt`,
        `OfLong`, `OfShort` and `OfBoolean`; `Void` is a void result; and `Reference(tag, id)`
        stays an identifier with its runtime tag, for `Halt` and `Variable.Snapshot` to
        interpret. `Halt#assign` takes a `Value` of the variable's erased type.
    entry .inspectable   renders a primitive by its own notation, a reference as `tag＠id`, and null as `null`
        Parameterised over the enumeration's case types, since `Inspectable` is invariant: an
        instance for `Value` alone would not cover `OfInt` or `Reference`.
  entry .Modifier   the constraints on an event request, each with its JDWP modkind
    detail
        `Count(count)` (1) fires the request that many times then expires it; `ThreadOnly`
        (3), `ClassOnly` (4), `ClassMatch(pattern)` (5) and `ClassExclude(pattern)` (6)
        restrict by thread or class, the patterns being an exact name or one bounded by a
        single `*`; `LocationOnly(location)` (7) places a breakpoint; `ExceptionOnly(cls,
        caught, uncaught)` (8) selects exceptions, the empty reference meaning any class;
        `FieldOnly(cls, field)` (9) names a watched field; `Step(thread, size, depth)` (10)
        describes a step; and `SourceNameMatch(pattern)` (12) restricts class-prepare events by
        source file, where the VM supports it. JDWP applies a `Count` only to events which pass
        the modifiers before it, so it goes last.
  entry .Version   the VM's version reply: a description, the JDWP major and minor, and the VM's version and name
    entry #description   the VM's own description text
    entry #major   the JDWP major version
    entry #minor   the JDWP minor version
    entry #vmVersion   the VM's version string
    entry #vmName   the VM's name
  entry .ClassInfo   a loaded reference type: its tag, identifier, JNI signature and status
    entry #tag   class, interface or array
    entry #cls   the reference type
    entry #signature   the JNI signature, `Lcom/example/Main;`
    entry #status   the class's status bits: verified, prepared, initialised, error
  entry .MethodInfo   a method of a reference type: its identifier, name, JNI signature and modifiers
    entry #method   the method
    entry #name   the method's name, `<init>` for a constructor
    entry #signature   the JNI signature, `(I)Ljava/lang/String;`
    entry #modifiers   the access flags; bit 0x40 marks a bridge, 0x1000 a synthetic method
  entry .FieldInfo   a field of a reference type: its identifier, name, JNI signature and modifiers
    entry #field   the field
    entry #name   the field's name
    entry #signature   the JNI signature
    entry #modifiers   the access flags; bit 0x8 marks a static field
  entry .LineEntry   one line-table row: a bytecode index and the source line which begins there
    entry #index   the bytecode index
    entry #line   the source line
  entry .LineTable   a method's line table: its first and last bytecode indices and its rows
    detail
        `start` is −1 for a method with no executable code (abstract, native). The source line
        of a location is the `line` of the last entry whose `index` is at most the location's.
    entry #start   the lowest bytecode index, or −1 for a method with no code
    entry #end   the highest bytecode index
    entry #lines   the rows, in bytecode order
  entry .SlotInfo   a local-variable table row: the slot's bytecode range, name, JNI signature and slot number
    detail
        A slot is live at a bytecode index within `[index, index + length)`; `Halt#variables`
        filters by this.
    entry #index   the bytecode index at which the slot becomes live
    entry #name   the variable's name
    entry #signature   the JNI signature
    entry #length   the number of bytecode indices over which the slot is live
    entry #slot   the slot number, as `Connection#slotValues` takes it
  entry .VariableTable   a method's local-variable table: its argument count and slots
    entry #argCount   the number of slots the method's parameters occupy
    entry #slots   the slots, as `SlotInfo`s
  entry .ThreadStatus   a thread's status: its run state and whether it is suspended
    entry #threadStatus   zombie (0), running (1), sleeping (2), monitor (3) or wait (4)
    entry #suspendStatus   1 when the thread is suspended
  entry .Invocation   the outcome of a method invoked in the debuggee: its return value and the exception it threw
    entry #result   the return value, `Void` for a void method
    entry #exception   the exception thrown, or the null reference when the method returned normally
  entry .Capabilities   the flags this library consults from `VirtualMachine.CapabilitiesNew`
    detail
        Decoded by `Connection#capabilitiesNew` and memoized by `Debug#capabilities`; a short
        reply from an old VM leaves the missing flags false. The constructor order is
        `canWatchFieldModification`, `canWatchFieldAccess`, `canGetBytecodes`,
        `canGetSyntheticAttribute`, `canPopFrames`, `canGetSourceDebugExtension`,
        `canUseSourceNameFilters`, `canGetConstantPool`.
    entry #canWatchFieldModification   whether `Debug#watch` on a write is possible
    entry #canWatchFieldAccess   whether `Debug#watch` on a read is possible
    entry #canGetBytecodes   whether a method's bytecode can be read, which the step filter uses to recognise forwarders
    entry #canGetSyntheticAttribute   whether the VM reports synthetic members
    entry #canPopFrames   whether `Halt#pop` is possible
    entry #canGetSourceDebugExtension   whether SMAPs can be read, which inline-aware positions and steps need
    entry #canUseSourceNameFilters   whether a class-prepare request can be narrowed by source file
    entry #canGetConstantPool   whether a class's constant pool can be read, which the step filter needs
  entry .Reader   decodes a packet payload: big-endian, with identifier reads at the negotiated widths
    detail
        A `Reader(data, sizes)` walks a materialised `Data` payload from the start; every read
        advances its position, so it is a `caps.Mutable` class held as `Reader^` and used by
        one decoder at a time, in order. `Connection#request` returns one over a reply's
        payload, and `Event.composite` decodes a composite from one.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val sizes: Jdwp.IdSizes = Jdwp.IdSizes(8, 8, 8, 8, 8)
        val payload: Data = Jdwp.Writer(sizes).int(3).string(t"main").data
        val reader: Jdwp.Reader^ = Jdwp.Reader(payload, sizes)
        val count: Int = reader.int()
        val name: Text = reader.string()
        ```
    entry #remaining   the bytes not yet read
    entry #byte   reads one byte
    entry #boolean   reads one byte as a boolean
    entry #short   reads a big-endian 16-bit integer
    entry #char   reads a big-endian 16-bit character
    entry #int   reads a big-endian 32-bit integer
    entry #long   reads a big-endian 64-bit integer
    entry #float   reads a 32-bit IEEE float
    entry #double   reads a 64-bit IEEE double
    entry #id   reads an identifier of the given width in bytes
    entry #objectId   reads an `ObjectId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #threadId   reads a `ThreadId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #threadGroupId   reads a `ThreadGroupId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #stringId   reads a `StringId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #classLoaderId   reads a `ClassLoaderId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #referenceTypeId   reads a `ReferenceTypeId` at the negotiated reference-type width
    entry #methodId   reads a `MethodId` at the negotiated method width
    entry #fieldId   reads a `FieldId` at the negotiated field width
    entry #frameId   reads a `FrameId` at the negotiated frame width
    entry #string   reads a length-prefixed JDWP string, in JNI modified UTF-8
        The null character arrives as `C0 80` and a character outside the basic multilingual
        plane as two three-byte sequences, which pass through as the surrogate pair they
        encode; a strict UTF-8 decoder would reject both.
    entry #modifiedUtf8   reads a modified-UTF-8 run of a length already read, as a classfile constant pool stores one
    entry #skip   advances past a number of bytes
    entry #location   reads a `Location`: a type tag, reference type, method and index
    entry #value   reads a tagged `Value`
    entry #untaggedValue   reads a `Value` whose tag the caller already knows
    entry #arrayRegion   reads an array region: one tag for the region, a count, then the elements
        A primitive region packs untagged values of its one type; an object region carries
        ordinarily tagged values, since the elements' runtime types may differ.
  entry .Writer   marshals a packet payload: fluent, big-endian, identifiers at the negotiated widths
    detail
        A `Writer(sizes)` accumulates bytes; each write returns the writer, and `#data`
        snapshots what has been written. `Connection#request` lends one to the caller's
        `write` function.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val sizes: Jdwp.IdSizes = Jdwp.IdSizes(8, 8, 8, 8, 8)
        val body: Data = Jdwp.Writer(sizes).byte(2).byte(2).int(1).modifier(Jdwp.Modifier.Count(1)).data
        ```
    entry #byte   writes one byte
    entry #boolean   writes a boolean as one byte
    entry #short   writes a big-endian 16-bit integer
    entry #char   writes a big-endian 16-bit character
    entry #int   writes a big-endian 32-bit integer
    entry #long   writes a big-endian 64-bit integer
    entry #float   writes a 32-bit IEEE float
    entry #double   writes a 64-bit IEEE double
    entry #id   writes an identifier at the given width in bytes
    entry #objectId   writes an `ObjectId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #threadId   writes a `ThreadId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #threadGroupId   writes a `ThreadGroupId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #stringId   writes a `StringId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #classLoaderId   writes a `ClassLoaderId` at the negotiated object width
    entry #referenceTypeId   writes a `ReferenceTypeId` at the negotiated reference-type width
    entry #methodId   writes a `MethodId` at the negotiated method width
    entry #fieldId   writes a `FieldId` at the negotiated field width
    entry #frameId   writes a `FrameId` at the negotiated frame width
    entry #string   writes a length-prefixed JDWP string, in JNI modified UTF-8
    entry #location   writes a `Location`
    entry #value   writes a `Value` with its tag byte
    entry #untaggedValue   writes a `Value` with no tag, for a field write or a primitive array region
    entry #modifier   writes a `Modifier`: its modkind byte then its payload
    entry #bytes   appends raw bytes
    entry #data   the bytes written so far, as `Data`
  entry .Event   the events a VM sends back, decoded from a Composite command
    detail
        `VmStart(request, thread)`, `VmDeath(request)`, `ThreadStart` and `ThreadDeath`
        (`request, thread`), `Breakpoint`, `SingleStep`, `MethodEntry` and `MethodExit`
        (`request, thread, location`), `Thrown(request, thread, location, exception,
        catchLocation)`, `FieldAccess(request, thread, location, tag, cls, field, obj)`,
        `FieldModified(…, obj, incoming)`, `ClassPrepared(request, thread, tag, cls, signature,
        status)` and `Unknown(kind, request)`. Only the kinds this debugger requests are
        modelled in full: an unrecognised kind decodes as `Unknown` and ends the composite,
        since its payload length is unknown and the stream cannot be resynced within the
        packet (the next packet is unaffected, being length-framed). `request` is the id of the
        event request which caused the event, `0` for an automatic one such as `VmStart`.
    entry .Composite   one Composite command: a suspend policy and the events it carries
      detail
          JDWP suspends once per composite, by its `policy`, and the dispatcher resumes it
          once; a composite no handler claims is handed to `Debug#events` whole.
      entry #policy   how much of the VM the composite suspended
      entry #events   the events, in the order the VM sent them
    entry .composite   decodes a composite from a reader positioned at a Composite command's payload
  entry .Packet   a complete JDWP packet: id, flags, error code, command set, command and body
    detail
        The 4-byte length prefix counts the whole packet, header included; the `flags` high bit
        marks a reply, which carries a 2-byte error code where a command carries a 1-byte
        command set and a 1-byte command. `Packet.command` frames a command, `Packet.reply` a
        reply (as a fake VM in a test does), and `Packet.decode` reads one back.
    entry #id   the packet id, which correlates a reply with its command
    entry #flags   the flag byte; the high bit marks a reply
    entry #code   a reply's error code, zero for success; zero for a command
    entry #commandSet   a command's set number; zero for a reply
    entry #command   a command's number within its set; zero for a reply
    entry #body   the payload after the eleven-byte header
    entry #reply   whether the packet is a reply
    entry .headerLength   eleven: the bytes before the payload
    entry .command   frames a command packet from an id, a set, a command number and a body
    entry .reply   frames a reply packet from an id, an error code (non-zero for failure) and a body
    entry .decode   decodes one complete packet, exactly its own length of bytes
  entry .Connection   the wire-level client: frames and correlates packets over a duplex channel
    detail
        A `Connection` owns the pumps a session needs: a single writer task drains outgoing
        packets so writes never interleave, a reader task reassembles length-framed packets and
        fulfils the promise each reply settles or enqueues each composite, and a dispatcher task
        runs the registered handlers — never the reader, so a handler's own command cannot wait
        on the task which must deliver its reply. A handler which issues a command pumps the
        composite queue re-entrantly while it waits, since an invoked method can raise an
        event (a class prepare during linking) which suspends the very thread running the
        invoke, and only the dispatcher can settle it.

        It is a `SharedCapability`, driven by its three tasks and the session's thread at once,
        with every piece of its state synchronised. `Debug` wraps one; it is reached directly
        for the commands the session does not expose, as the `Evaluator` does to allocate an
        array, intern a string and invoke `defineClass` in the debuggee. The command methods
        are named for the specification's, grouped by command set, and each takes a
        `Tactic[Debugger.Error]` through which a reply's error code is raised.
    refer vivisection.Debug
    entry #sizes   the identifier widths negotiated at the start of the session
    entry #request   frames and issues a command, handing back a reader over the reply's payload
        `request(set, command)(write)` lends a `Writer` to `write`, sends the packet, awaits
        the reply and returns a `Reader^` positioned at its payload. A failed reply raises a
        `Debugger.Error` with the VM's reason and the detail `command (set, command)`, and an
        empty reader is returned so that decoding degrades rather than crashes.
    entry #version   `VirtualMachine.Version` (1, 1): the VM's version
    entry #allClasses   `VirtualMachine.AllClasses` (1, 3): every loaded reference type
        Enumerates the whole VM, so it must not be called while a thread stands suspended
        holding a class-loading lock (at a `ClassPrepare` event): use `Connection#classesBySignature`.
    entry #classesBySignature   `VirtualMachine.ClassesBySignature` (1, 2): the loaded classes with exactly one JNI signature
        One per defining loader. Safe at a `ClassPrepare` event, unlike `Connection#allClasses`.
    entry #allThreads   `VirtualMachine.AllThreads` (1, 4): every thread
    entry #suspendAll   `VirtualMachine.Suspend` (1, 8): suspends every thread
    entry #resumeAll   `VirtualMachine.Resume` (1, 9): resumes every thread
    entry #dispose   `VirtualMachine.Dispose` (1, 6): ends the debugging session, clearing every request
    entry #createString   `VirtualMachine.CreateString` (1, 11): interns a string in the debuggee, for an invocation
    entry #capabilitiesNew   `VirtualMachine.CapabilitiesNew` (1, 17): the flags named in `Capabilities`
    entry #signature   `ReferenceType.Signature` (2, 1): a type's JNI signature
    entry #sourceFile   `ReferenceType.SourceFile` (2, 7): the source file a type was compiled from, by basename
    entry #classLoader   `ReferenceType.ClassLoader` (2, 2): the loader which defined a type, or the null reference for a bootstrap class
    entry #smap   a class's parsed SMAP, memoized for the session, or `Unset` if it has none
        Parsed by digression from `Connection#sourceDebugExtension`; the absence is memoized too, so a
        class with no debug extension (or a VM without `canGetSourceDebugExtension`) costs one
        round trip, ever. This is what makes positions and steps inline-aware.
    entry #sourceDebugExtension   `ReferenceType.SourceDebugExtension` (2, 12): the raw SMAP text
    entry #methods   `ReferenceType.Methods` (2, 5): a type's methods
    entry #fields   `ReferenceType.Fields` (2, 4): a type's own fields, inherited ones excluded
    entry #constantPool   `ReferenceType.ConstantPool` (2, 18): the names a class's method references carry, memoized
        Returns the library's own parsed pool, which the step filter consults to recognise a
        forwarder by its callee's name; it is package-private, so a caller outside the library
        can only pass it along. Needs `canGetConstantPool`; without it the pool is empty.
    entry #bytecodes   `Method.Bytecodes` (6, 3): a method's bytecode, as a reader at its first opcode
        Needs `canGetBytecodes`.
    entry #superclass   `ClassType.Superclass` (3, 1): the immediate superclass, or the empty reference for `Object`
        Walking it reaches inherited fields, which `Connection#fields` omits; `Halt#exceptionInfo` walks
        it to find `Throwable`'s `detailMessage`.
    entry #invokeStatic   `ClassType.InvokeMethod` (3, 3): invokes a static method on a suspended thread
        Single-threaded: every other thread stays suspended for the duration, so a method which
        needs another thread would deadlock. Returns the `Invocation` with its result and any
        exception.
    entry #newInstance   `ClassType.NewInstance` (3, 4): constructs an object on a suspended thread
    entry #newArray   `ArrayType.NewInstance` (4, 1): allocates an array of a given length in the debuggee
    entry #lineTable   `Method.LineTable` (6, 1): a method's bytecode-to-line mapping
    entry #variableTable   `Method.VariableTable` (6, 2): a method's local-variable table, or `Unset` without debug information
        A class compiled without local-variable debug information makes the VM answer
        `AbsentInformation` (101), decoded as `Unset` rather than raised, since callers degrade
        to captured and field state.
    entry #referenceType   `ObjectReference.ReferenceType` (9, 1): an object's runtime type, with its tag
    entry #fieldValues   `ObjectReference.GetValues` (9, 2): the values of an object's fields
    entry #setFieldValues   `ObjectReference.SetValues` (9, 3): writes an object's fields, untagged
    entry #invokeMethod   `ObjectReference.InvokeMethod` (9, 6): invokes an instance method on a suspended thread
        Single-threaded, as `Connection#invokeStatic`. JDWP invocation ignores access control, which is
        how the `Evaluator` calls a class loader's protected `defineClass`.
    entry #reflectedType   `ClassObjectReference.ReflectedType` (17, 1): the reference type a `java.lang.Class` object stands for
    entry #stringValue   `StringReference.Value` (10, 1): a string object's text
    entry #threadName   `ThreadReference.Name` (11, 1): a thread's name
    entry #suspendThread   `ThreadReference.Suspend` (11, 2): suspends one thread
    entry #resumeThread   `ThreadReference.Resume` (11, 3): resumes one thread
    entry #threadStatus   `ThreadReference.Status` (11, 4): a thread's run and suspend status
    entry #frameCount   `ThreadReference.FrameCount` (11, 7): the depth of a suspended thread's stack
    entry #frames   `ThreadReference.Frames` (11, 6): a range of a suspended thread's frames, with their locations
    entry #arrayLength   `ArrayReference.Length` (13, 1): an array's length
    entry #arrayValues   `ArrayReference.GetValues` (13, 2): a range of an array's elements
    entry #setArrayValues   `ArrayReference.SetValues` (13, 3): writes a range of an array's elements, untagged
    entry #eventRequestSet   `EventRequest.Set` (15, 1): installs an event request, returning its id
        Takes the `EventKind`, the `SuspendPolicy` and the `Modifier`s; the id is what
        `Connection#eventRequestClear` and `Debug#clear` take.
    entry #eventRequestClear   `EventRequest.Clear` (15, 2): cancels an event request by kind and id
    entry #slotValues   `StackFrame.GetValues` (16, 1): the values of a frame's slots, each requested with its type's tag
    entry #setSlotValues   `StackFrame.SetValues` (16, 2): writes a frame's slots
    entry #thisObject   `StackFrame.ThisObject` (16, 3): a frame's `this`, the null object in a static or native frame
    entry #popFrames   `StackFrame.PopFrames` (16, 4): discards frames up to and including one, so resuming re-executes the call
        Needs `canPopFrames` and a thread suspended by an event.
    entry .Reply   the outcome of a command: `Ok(data)` with the payload, or `Failed(code)` with the VM's error code
        A dedicated type rather than an `Either`, so the reply channel carries no
        `Nothing`-typed payload. `Failed(-1)` is a lost connection.
    entry .Slot   holds a breakpoint handler, as `Halt => Unit`, in the connection's registry
      hidden
      detail
          Not for direct use: a holder which keeps a session-capturing handler out of the
          capture-tracked world, since the handler lives and dies with the session. Its `run`
          is the handler.
    entry .PrepareSlot   holds a class-prepare handler, as `Event.ClassPrepared => Unit`, in the registry
      hidden
      detail
          Not for direct use: the class-prepare counterpart of `Slot`, receiving the raw event
          since a prepared class has no stopped frame to inspect. Its `run` is the handler.

entry .evaluator   opens a compiler session over the debuggee's classpath and lends an `Evaluator` at a stop
    An extension on `Halt`, in `vivisection.eval`. `stop.evaluator(classpath) { eval ?=> … }`
    starts a warm `Scalac[3.9]` session (with `-experimental`, since the debuggee's own types
    and `Inspectable` use experimental features) over the `LocalClasspath` the debuggee was
    launched with, builds a `Purview` over it, and lends an `Evaluator` for the block. The
    session and the evaluator both die with the block, and neither can outlive the stop that
    lent the halt. It needs a `Monitor`, a `System`, a `CompileEvent is Loggable`, and tactics
    for `Async.Error`, `Compiler.Error` and `Debugger.Error`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    def total(classpath: LocalClasspath)(using Debug^, Monitor, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Unit =
      debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(42)): stop ?=>
        stop.evaluator(classpath): eval ?=>
          Out.println(eval.inspect(t"total"))
          eval(t"total + 1")
    ```

    The classpath must be the one the debuggee's classes were compiled on, TASTy included: the
    evaluator compiles against it, and `Purview` reads declared types from its TASTy. Under a
    test host, that is `LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Main.type])`, not the `java.class.path`
    property.

entry .Evaluator   evaluates Scala expressions at a suspended frame, by compiling and injecting a class
  detail
      An `Evaluator` mirrors the stopped frame's visible locals as the constructor parameters of
      a freshly-named synthetic class in the frame's own package, compiles it against the
      debuggee's classpath in the warm compiler session lent by `evaluator`, ships every
      classfile into the debuggee as a `byte[]` handed to the frame's class loader's
      `defineClass` (JDWP has no define-class command, and its invocation ignores access
      control), instantiates the class with the locals' live values, and invokes its `run` on
      the stopped thread. So `total + 1` in a handler means what it would mean at that line of
      the source.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.{Variable as _, *}
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem

      def evaluate(classpath: LocalClasspath)(using Debug^, Monitor, Tactic[Debugger.Error]): Unit =
        debug.breakpoint(t"Main.scala", Ordinal.uniary(42)): stop ?=>
          stop.evaluator(classpath): eval ?=>
            eval(t"total + 1") match
              case vivisection.Variable.Snapshot.Str(_, text) => Out.println(text)
              case other                                      => Out.println(other.inspect)

            eval.assign(t"total", t"total*2")
      ```

      Each local is typed at its *declared* type where `Purview` can recover it from TASTy —
      an opaque `Port` rather than the `Int` it erases to — so `inspect` selects the
      `Inspectable` instance the programmer's own code would, and `assign` lets the compiler
      enforce assignability. Only a method's value parameters and its body's `val`s are
      resolved this way; anything else keeps its erased type. An `Evaluator` is an
      `ExclusiveCapability`, confined to the `evaluator` block. Every synthetic class is named
      distinctly, since a compiler session's retained symbol table forbids redefining a name.
  refer vivisection.evaluator
  refer vivisection.Purview
  refer vivisection.Halt
  entry #apply   evaluates an expression and returns its `String.valueOf` form as a snapshot
      `eval(expression)` compiles `String.valueOf((expression): Any)`, runs it, and returns a
      `Variable.Snapshot.Str` with the text — one decode path for every result type — or a
      `Primitive` if the invocation produced no string (a void or failed run).
  entry #assign   evaluates an expression at a variable's declared type and writes the result into it
      `assign(binding, expression)` finds the visible variable named `binding`, types the
      synthetic `run` at its declared (or, failing that, erased) type so the compiler enforces
      assignability and returns a primitive as a primitive, and writes the result through
      `Halt#assign`, routed by the variable's provenance. A frontend's variable edit.
  entry #completion   the synthetic source a completion host should typecheck, and the fragment's offset in it
      `completion(fragment)` stages — without compiling — the same class `apply` would, with
      the console fragment as the `run` body at `Any`, and returns the source with the offset at
      which the fragment begins. No compiler runs here: the host typechecks the source at a
      caret past the offset with a typechecking `Highlight` over the debuggee's classpath and
      rebases the replacement span by the same offset, so completion on a local resolves
      against its declared type, extension methods and givens included. `Unset` with no top
      frame.
  entry #elaborations   the call sites of the frame's method which the compiler elaborated, from the debuggee's TASTy
      Each `prophesy.Elaboration` records what the typer inserted at a call — inferred type
      arguments and synthesized `using` arguments — read from the unpickled tree, so it reflects
      what actually runs. The raw material of a hover.
  entry #variables   the frame's visible variables, each with its declared static type where TASTy supplies it
      `Halt#variables` with `Variable#static` filled in through `Purview#rendered`: the
      stenography rendering of each parameter's and body-local's declared type, capture sets
      included. A binding whose type cannot be recovered keeps `Unset` there and its erased type
      in `erased`.
  entry #inspect   renders a visible binding through its `Inspectable` instance, resolved and run in the debuggee
      `inspect(binding)` evaluates `(binding).inspect` with `spectacular` imported. Because the
      synthetic class types the binding at its declared type, the summon is static — the
      instance the programmer's own code would pick — so an opaque type renders through its
      own notation rather than its representation's, and an array as `⦋…⦌` rather than its
      `toString`.
  entry .Injector   defines classfiles in a running debuggee over JDWP
    detail
        Built by the evaluator for one evaluation, over the connection, the stopped thread and
        the target class loader. Each classfile is shipped as a `byte[]` allocated and filled
        over the wire, then passed to the loader's protected `defineClass`; linking is lazy, so
        order does not matter. Not constructed directly.
    entry #define   defines one classfile by binary name, returning the reference type of the loaded class
        Recovered from the `java.lang.Class` object `defineClass` hands back, so no lookup by
        name is needed (the freshly-defined class may not yet appear in `allClasses`). `Unset`
        when the define returned nothing.

entry .Purview   recovers the declared Scala types of a frame's bindings from the debuggee's compiled TASTy
  detail
      A `Purview(classpath)` holds a standalone compiler context over the debuggee's
      `LocalClasspath`, warmed once: the compiler's own symbol loader reads the classes' TASTy
      off the classpath, and a binding's declared type is then read straight from its symbol —
      a method's value parameters — or from its unpickled tree, for the `val`s in its body (a
      later binding of a name shadows an earlier one, as at a stop). This is what lets a value
      render through the instance its *declared* type selects: an opaque type through its own
      `Inspectable` rather than its underlying representation's.

      Every method degrades to an empty result on any failure — a class not on the classpath,
      TASTy the compiler cannot read — since a debugger reads types opportunistically and must
      never fail the frame. Types printed as `proscenium.Int` are normalised to the plain names
      the synthetic evaluation class compiles under.
  refer vivisection.Evaluator
  entry #parameters   the compile-usable source type of each binding of a method, keyed by name
      The compiler's own printed form, valid resolvable Scala, which the `Evaluator` uses to
      type the synthetic class's parameters. `className` is the demangled runtime class
      (`com.example.Main`) and `methodName` the method.
  entry #elaborations   the call sites in a method's body the typer elaborated, as `prophesy.Elaboration`s
      Inferred type arguments and synthesized `using` arguments, read from the unpickled tree
      with its source positions, so written arguments are told from inferred ones.
  entry #rendered   the human-facing rendering of each binding's declared type, through stenography
      Capture-set-aware and source-accurate, keyed by name: what a debugger shows as
      `Variable#static`.

entry .Namer   names an inline position from the TASTy beside the debuggee's classfiles
  detail
      A `Namer(classpath)` answers, for a `ScalaClass`-stratum class name and an origin
      position recorded in a class's SMAP, the definition the programmer wrote there — owner
      and name, demangled as a stack trace renders them — so an inline frame can be named for
      the method it came from rather than its class. A classloader over the launch classpath
      fetches `.tasty` resources only; it never defines debuggee classes in the debugger's JVM.
      Lookups are memoized per class, absences included; a miss walks outwards through
      `$`-joined enclosing classes, since nested and module classes pickle under their
      top-level class. The DAP adapter builds one at `launch`.
  entry #define   the definition at a line of a class's source, as `owner.name`, or `Unset`
      `define(cls, path, line)` takes the raw class name a `Halt.Position` carries in `cls`,
      the source path, and the line. `Unset` when the class has no TASTy on the classpath, when
      its TASTy was compiled from a different path than the SMAP records, or when no definition
      covers the line.

entry .Dap   the Debug Adapter Protocol: a stdio server over a session, and the protocol's message types
  detail
      `Dap.listen` serves a debug session to an editor — VS Code, or any other which speaks the
      protocol — over `Content-Length`-framed JSON on the standard streams. The editor's
      `launch` (by main class and classpath) or `attach` (by port) opens the session;
      `setBreakpoints`, `setFunctionBreakpoints`, `setExceptionBreakpoints` and
      `setDataBreakpoints` place breakpoints before the program runs; `configurationDone`
      resumes it; `threads`, `stackTrace`, `scopes` and `variables` inspect a stop;
      `continue`, `next`, `stepIn`, `stepOut` and `pause` drive threads; `evaluate`,
      `setVariable`, `setExpression`, `completions` and `exceptionInfo` go through the
      `Evaluator`; and `restartFrame` pops a frame. Inline-aware positions appear as `subtle`
      frames named for the definition the programmer wrote, where the launch classpath's TASTy
      can name them.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      def serve(): Unit = supervise:
        Dap.listen()
      ```

      The rest of the object is the protocol's message model, the subset this adapter speaks:
      argument types are `Json.Decodable` (they arrive), body types `Json.Encodable` (they
      leave), each with its given in its companion, and an `Unset` member is simply absent on
      the wire. Field names mirror the specification's exactly, `camelCase` and the
      reserved-word `type` included. They are of use to a program which drives or fakes a DAP
      client, as the library's tests do; a program debugging a JVM uses `Debug` directly.
  refer vivisection.Debug
  refer vivisection.Debuggee
  entry .listen   serves the protocol over the ambient standard streams until the input is exhausted
      The canonical stdio transport a frontend launches. All outgoing traffic flows through one
      writer task, so responses and events never interleave, and each request is handled in
      arrival order on the calling thread. `observer` sees every message's raw text in both
      directions (it runs on the writer task, so it may capture only shared capabilities). The
      `Stdio` is consumed: the writer task owns the standard streams, and the caller may not use
      them after calling `listen`. Needs the launch givens of `Debuggee.Sessional`, a `System`,
      a `CompileEvent is Loggable`, and tactics for `Async.Error`, `Framing.Error` (a malformed
      frame) and `Truncation.Error`. A client which drops the connection without a `disconnect`
      still releases the debuggee when the input ends.
  entry .Envelope   any DAP message, read only for the members which decide how it is handled
    detail
        `seq`, `type`, `command` and `event`, all optional, so every message decodes;
        `Dap.envelope` reads one from any JSON, defaulting to an empty envelope.
    entry #seq   the message's sequence number
    entry #type   `request`, `response` or `event`
    entry #command   a request's command name
    entry #event   an event's name
    entry .decodable   reads an envelope from JSON
  entry .envelope   the envelope of any JSON message; an empty one if it does not decode
  entry .response   builds a successful response to a request, with a sequence number and an optional body
  entry .failure   builds a failed response to a request, with a sequence number and a message
  entry .event   builds an event message by name, with a sequence number and an optional body
  entry .ExceptionFilter   an exception breakpoint filter the adapter advertises: its id, label and default
    entry #filter   the filter's id: `uncaught` or `caught`
    entry #label   the label shown to the user
    entry #default   whether the filter starts enabled
    entry .encodable   writes a filter to JSON
  entry .exceptionFilters   the two filters advertised: `uncaught` (on by default) and `caught`
  entry .Capabilities   what the adapter advertises in its `initialize` response
    detail
        Configuration-done, function, data and exception breakpoints, `setVariable`,
        `setExpression`, `exceptionInfo`, `restartFrame`, completions (triggered by `.`) and
        evaluate-for-hovers, all `true` by default; an absent flag reads as false.
    entry #supportsConfigurationDoneRequest   the client should send `configurationDone` after its breakpoints
    entry #supportsFunctionBreakpoints   `setFunctionBreakpoints` is handled
    entry #supportsDataBreakpoints   `dataBreakpointInfo` and `setDataBreakpoints` are handled
    entry #supportsSetVariable   `setVariable` is handled
    entry #supportsSetExpression   `setExpression` is handled
    entry #supportsExceptionInfoRequest   `exceptionInfo` is handled
    entry #supportsRestartFrame   `restartFrame` is handled
    entry #supportsCompletionsRequest   `completions` is handled
    entry #supportsEvaluateForHovers   `evaluate` with a `hover` context is handled
    entry #completionTriggerCharacters   the characters which trigger completion: `.`
    entry #exceptionBreakpointFilters   the filters offered: `Dap.exceptionFilters`
    entry .encodable   writes the capabilities to JSON
  entry .LaunchArguments   a `launch` request's arguments: a main class and a classpath, mirroring `Debuggee`
    detail
        Adapter-defined by the specification. The adapter launches `java -classpath
        <classpath> <mainClass>` with the agent on a free port, suspended at startup.
    entry #mainClass   the class whose `main` to run
    entry #classpath   the classpath, as `java.class.path` text
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .AttachArguments   an `attach` request's arguments: a port, and optionally a host name
    entry #port   the port the VM's agent listens on
    entry #hostName   the host, `localhost` by default
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .Source   a source reference: a name and a path, either optional
    entry #name   the file's basename
    entry #path   the file's path
    entry .decodable   reads a source from JSON
    entry .encodable   writes a source to JSON
  entry .SourceBreakpointSpec   one requested source breakpoint: its line
    entry #line   the one-based line
    entry .decodable   reads a spec from JSON
  entry .SetBreakpointsArguments   a `setBreakpoints` request: the source and every breakpoint wanted in it
    detail
        The protocol's replace semantics: the request's breakpoints replace every breakpoint
        previously set in that source.
    entry #source   the source file
    entry #breakpoints   the breakpoints wanted, as `SourceBreakpointSpec`s
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .SetExceptionBreakpointsArguments   a `setExceptionBreakpoints` request: the enabled filter ids
    entry #filters   `uncaught`, `caught`, both or neither
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .FunctionBreakpointSpec   one requested function breakpoint: a `Class.method` name
    entry #name   the runtime class and method, `com.example.Ledger.deposit`
    entry .decodable   reads a spec from JSON
  entry .SetFunctionBreakpointsArguments   a `setFunctionBreakpoints` request: every function breakpoint wanted
    entry #breakpoints   the breakpoints wanted, as `FunctionBreakpointSpec`s
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .DataBreakpointInfoArguments   a `dataBreakpointInfo` request: a variable name and its scope reference
    detail
        Answered with a `DataBreakpointInfoBody` whose `dataId` is `owner:field` for a member
        field, the only kind of variable a data breakpoint supports.
    entry #name   the variable's name
    entry #variablesReference   the scope the variable is in
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .DataBreakpointSpec   one requested data breakpoint: a `dataId` of the form `class:field`
    entry #dataId   the id a `dataBreakpointInfo` response gave
    entry .decodable   reads a spec from JSON
  entry .SetDataBreakpointsArguments   a `setDataBreakpoints` request: every data breakpoint wanted
    entry #breakpoints   the breakpoints wanted, as `DataBreakpointSpec`s
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .ThreadArguments   a request naming a thread: `next`, `stepIn`, `stepOut`, `pause`, `exceptionInfo`
    entry #threadId   the adapter's handle for the thread
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .StackTraceArguments   a `stackTrace` request: the thread, and optionally a frame range
    entry #threadId   the adapter's handle for the thread
    entry #startFrame   the first frame wanted
    entry #levels   how many frames are wanted
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .FrameArguments   a request naming a frame: `scopes` and `restartFrame`
    entry #frameId   the adapter's handle for the frame
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .VariablesArguments   a `variables` request: the scope or structure to expand
    entry #variablesReference   the handle a `scopes` or `variables` response gave
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .SetVariableArguments   a `setVariable` request: the scope, the variable's name and its new value as text
    detail
        The value is parsed at the variable's erased type: the primitives, plus a fresh remote
        string. Anything richer belongs to `setExpression`.
    entry #variablesReference   the scope the variable is in
    entry #name   the variable's name
    entry #value   the new value, as text
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .EvaluateArguments   an `evaluate` request: the expression, the frame, and the context it is asked in
    detail
        A `hover` context never runs debuggee code, since it fires on cursor movement: it
        answers only with a visible local's value and static type, or the elaboration of a call
        named in the stopped method. Every other context (`repl`, `watch`, absent) evaluates
        through the `Evaluator`.
    entry #expression   the expression
    entry #frameId   the frame to evaluate against
    entry #context   `hover`, `repl`, `watch`, or absent
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .CompletionsArguments   a `completions` request: the console text, the caret column and the frame
    detail
        `column` is a one-based character position within `text`; `line` is accepted but
        unused, since the adapter completes single-line console input.
    entry #text   the console text so far
    entry #column   the one-based caret position in `text`
    entry #frameId   the frame whose locals are in scope
    entry #line   accepted and unused
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .SetExpressionArguments   a `setExpression` request: a binding, an expression for its new value, and the frame
    entry #expression   the binding to assign to
    entry #value   the expression whose value to assign
    entry #frameId   the frame
    entry .decodable   reads the arguments from JSON
  entry .Breakpoint   a breakpoint as reported to the client: verified or not, with its id, line and message
    entry #verified   whether the breakpoint has bound to a location
    entry #id   the adapter's id for it
    entry #line   its line
    entry #message   why it is unverified, if given
    entry .encodable   writes a breakpoint to JSON
  entry .BreakpointsBody   the body of a `setBreakpoints`-family response: one `Breakpoint` per request
    entry #breakpoints   the breakpoints, in the order requested
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .DataBreakpointInfoBody   the body of a `dataBreakpointInfo` response: the data id, a description and the access types
    entry #dataId   `owner:field`, or `Unset` when the variable supports no data breakpoint
    entry #description   a description for the user, or the reason none is supported
    entry #accessTypes   `write`, the only access type supported
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .ThreadInfo   a thread as reported to the client: its handle and name
    entry #id   the adapter's handle
    entry #name   the thread's name
    entry .encodable   writes the thread to JSON
  entry .ThreadsBody   the body of a `threads` response
    entry #threads   every thread
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .StackFrame   a frame as reported to the client: handle, name, line, column, source and presentation hint
    detail
        One per *logical* position: a physical frame inside inlined code expands into its
        inline origins, hinted `subtle`, followed by the frame itself at its real line.
    entry #id   the adapter's handle
    entry #name   the method, or the inlined definition the programmer wrote
    entry #line   the source line
    entry #column   the column, always `0`
    entry #source   the source file, if known
    entry #presentationHint   `subtle` for an inline origin
    entry .encodable   writes the frame to JSON
  entry .StackTraceBody   the body of a `stackTrace` response: the frames and their total
    entry #stackFrames   the frames, innermost first
    entry #totalFrames   the number of frames
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .Scope   a variable scope: its name, its handle and whether expanding it is expensive
    entry #name   `Locals`, the one scope offered
    entry #variablesReference   the handle a `variables` request takes
    entry #expensive   always `false`
    entry .encodable   writes the scope to JSON
  entry .ScopesBody   the body of a `scopes` response
    entry #scopes   the scopes of the frame
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .VariableInfo   a variable as reported to the client: name, rendered value, expansion handle and type
    entry #name   the variable's name
    entry #value   its rendered value
    entry #variablesReference   a handle to expand it, or `0` for a leaf
    entry #type   its static type where recovered, else its erased type
    entry .encodable   writes the variable to JSON
  entry .VariablesBody   the body of a `variables` response
    entry #variables   the variables of the scope or structure
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .SetVariableBody   the body of a `setVariable` or `setExpression` response: the value as written
    entry #value   the new value, as text
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .EvaluateBody   the body of an `evaluate` response: the result and an expansion handle
    entry #result   the result, as text
    entry #variablesReference   a handle to expand it, or `0`
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .CompletionItem   one completion: its label, kind, and the span of the text it replaces
    detail
        `start` and `length` delimit the region of the request's `text` the completion
        replaces, in the same one-based units as the request's `column`; when omitted, the
        client inserts `label` at the requested column.
    entry #label   the completion text
    entry #type   `method`, `class`, `module`, `keyword` or `variable`
    entry #start   where the replaced region begins, one-based
    entry #length   how much is replaced
    entry .encodable   writes the item to JSON
  entry .CompletionsBody   the body of a `completions` response
    entry #targets   the completions
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .ExceptionInfoBody   the body of an `exceptionInfo` response: the exception's class, break mode and message
    entry #exceptionId   the exception's class name
    entry #breakMode   `always` for a caught exception, `unhandled` for an uncaught one
    entry #description   the exception's message
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .ContinueBody   the body of a `continue` response: whether every thread continued
    entry #allThreadsContinued   always `true`
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .StoppedBody   the body of a `stopped` event: the reason, the thread, and which breakpoints hit
    detail
        The reasons sent are `breakpoint`, `function breakpoint`, `data breakpoint`,
        `exception`, `step`, `pause` and `restart`.
    entry #reason   why the thread stopped
    entry #threadId   the adapter's handle for the thread
    entry #allThreadsStopped   whether every thread stopped; `false` for a step or pause
    entry #hitBreakpointIds   the ids of the breakpoints hit
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .ContinuedBody   the body of a `continued` event: the thread and whether every thread continued
    entry #threadId   the adapter's handle for the thread
    entry #allThreadsContinued   whether every thread continued
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .OutputBody   the body of an `output` event: the debuggee's output and its category
    entry #output   the text
    entry #category   `stdout` or `stderr`
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .ExitedBody   the body of an `exited` event: the debuggee's exit code
    entry #exitCode   the exit code
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
  entry .BreakpointEventBody   the body of a `breakpoint` event: a reason and the breakpoint's new state
    detail
        Sent with reason `changed` when a deferred source breakpoint binds, so the client marks
        it verified.
    entry #reason   `changed`
    entry #breakpoint   the breakpoint's new state
    entry .encodable   writes the body to JSON
